A||N||2||Seanh||Ball Joint won't seperate!||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-24-2001||08:04 PM||||I was starting on my car, and I am trying to seperate ball joints and remove the control arms. I am working on the drivers side first, as far as I got so far. I have Rodney's ball joint seperator tool also. I removed the cotter pins, and loosened the nuts 1 turn. I put a floor jack under the contol arm to support it once joints are seperated, but its not applying any pressure to the arm, just barly touching it. I inserted the seperator between the studs (following Rodney's supplied instructions) and tighted it down as much as I could. I finally heard the upper joint seperate, and confimed it was the upper joint by checking the nuts, and I couldn't turn the upper one, but still could turn the lower one. I lowered the jack a little bit, and continued to turn the tool. I turned it as far as I could possibly turn it by myself, and still can't hear (or feel) the lower joint seperate. What should I do? I wanted to get the arms off the car tonight, but it doesn't look like thats going to happen. Do you think I should just go buy a stupid pickle fork thing tomarrow and use it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bob Englert||06-24-2001||08:42 PM||||If you don't plan to reuse the lower ball joint then a pickle fork might do the trick.  If you do plan to reuse it I would soak the lower ball joint stud in a a lot of PB Blaster and then hit the top of the lower stud a few times with a medium hammer.  Sometime the hammer action on the stud will break it free.  <P>I have found that Rodney's tool works best, but they can sometimes be very stubborn.  It also scares the crap out of me when they break loose!  Tighten them a far as you dare, but be careful that you don't bend the ball joint stud.  <P>I would be soaking EVERYTHING with penetrating oil often while you are beginning the teardown.  The rear lower control arm bolts are usually the hardest to remove if the car has been exposed to road salt.  On my maroon 87 GT I had to grind off the heads of the rear lower control arm bolts on both sides because the bolt had frozen to the inner sleeve of the bushing.  <P>Coat the entire bolt length with anti-seize when reinstalling just in case you have to do it again some time.<P>Also be very careful with the front springs, they can be very dangerous if not handled carefully, particularly the WS6 springs.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodney||06-24-2001||09:00 PM||||Try hitting the casting where the stud goes through. Use a large drift and a big hammer. The extra impact usually does it. Garages actually have an attachment they put on and air chisel to do the same thing.||||reg||1
